  Follow-up diagnostic-matrix reevaluation (approved by the user on 2026-11-03)
    The earlier eight-row reduction established exact duplicate coverage. It did not establish
    that every remaining scene needed the full budget/resolution Cartesian product. This
    follow-up evaluates the 66 diagnostics by failure mode and meaningful axis interactions;
    the 30 budget and 24 continuity cases remain unchanged by this additional reduction.
    Current raw diagnostic traversal uses one shader binary with a dynamic budget. In
    liquids/refraction.glsl the budget selects only the loop limit; success returns immediately,
    and rejection/proposal logic within that prefix does not otherwise vary with the budget.
    Use budget 2 for ordinary successful scenes, so they must succeed within the lowest ceiling.
    Use budget 8 for rejection/early-stop scenes, allowing every available probe to expose an
    unsupported hit and proving early termination below the ceiling where asserted. Existing
    Budget tests independently retain all three ceilings, full/half tier selection, endpoint
    handling and fallback reuse. Actual compiled quality/resolution variants retain their
    separate production-liquid witnesses; these dynamic rows do not add compiled variants.
    Approved retained rows (scenario IDs remain unchanged; full/half are source resolutions):
      1 tilted:              budget 2, full. Representative normal-dependent raw success.
      2 shallow-plane:       budget 2, full and half. One-centimetre eligibility with both
                             direct reconstruction and reduced original-source support.
      3 adjacent-foreground: budget 2, full. Mixed-depth raw traversal retains valid support.
      4 sky:                 budget 8, full. Unavailable depth, reason and zero-color-read work.
      5 range-exhaustion:    budget 8, full. Stop after two evaluations despite a larger ceiling.
      6 edge-motion:         budget 2, full and half. Preserve all six columns, including the
                             center, and source-provenance/partial-edge interactions.
      7 range-fade:          budget 2, full. A valid long path retains full confidence.
      8 positive-view-z:     budget 8, full and half. Preserve the existing explicit hit assertion,
                             which is conditional on budget 8, and positive-Z direction checks.
      10 depth-discontinuity: budget 8, full and half. Maximal search must reject false geometry,
                             including the reduced support/depth-layer interaction.
      11 grazing:           budget 8, full. Representative unsupported geometric rejection.
    This is 14 rows, all found exactly once and passing in both existing Phase 2 TRX receipts.
    Their 24 logged pixel observations agree exactly between Debug and Release. Keep every
    assertion and edge column in each retained row. Scenario 0's standalone flat rows are unnecessary because
    scenario 6 at x=64 has identical textures, projection, default normal and shader path,
    and asserts the flat guarantees plus confidence. The diagnostic shader has no derivative
    or inter-pixel feedback dependency on the extra edge columns. Other rows retain one-pixel
    scissor/state-restoration coverage.
    The half-size adjacent-foreground case loses its only foreground pixel at (65,64): the
    farthest-valid 2x2 reduction selects the unchanged background at (64,64), producing the same
    reduced input pair as flat/edge-center. Full-size adjacent foreground remains selected;
    direct filter and reducer tests retain foreground exclusion and farthest-pair selection.
    Sky rejects depth 1 before source-resolution decoding. Tilted, range-exhaustion, long-path
    confidence and grazing use shared logic, with half reconstruction/coverage independently
    exercised by the retained near-interface, edge, positive-Z and discontinuity rows.
    Existing Phase2-water-coverage Debug/Release TRX observations agree exactly. Complete logs
    are budget-invariant in 17 of 22 scene/resolution groups. Sky evaluation counts vary 2/4/6;
    discontinuity counts vary 2/4/8; half positive-Z fails at budget 2 and succeeds in three
    evaluations at 4/8. Full positive-Z succeeds in two. The approved positive-Z rows retain
    budget 8 at both resolutions so their current success assertions remain active.
    Those observations support the source analysis; equal logs alone do not prove redundancy,
    and the diagnostic work counters are asserted rather than logged. The new policy is
    representative failure-mode sampling. It deliberately drops exhaustive scene×budget and
    scene×resolution interaction detection: a future conditional bug affecting only an omitted
    combination could escape this suite. Dedicated limit, decoder/filter and compiled-variant
    tests retain their distinct guarantees. New budget-dependent sampling logic or a reproduced
    omitted interaction would require reevaluating this selection.
    The user approved removal of 52 diagnostic rows: the primary inventory becomes 68 cases
    (14 diagnostic, 30 budget, 24 continuity). This does not remove any 201/61-motion observations,
    change tolerances or promise wall-time savings. Independent test review supports the
    sampling policy and its stated tradeoff. Implementation scope is the explicit 14-row
    diagnostic selection and cleanup of the unreachable scenario-0-only assertion branch.
    All remaining assertions and sampled coordinates must stay unchanged. Verification must
    establish exact approved membership, unchanged retained observations in Debug/Release,
    and source/shader identity for reused independent-witness and motion-sweep evidence.
