☑ Phase 3 — Separate atmosphere policy checks from repeated numerical construction
  Baseline and approved separation (2026-11-03)
    The unchanged AtmosphereScatteringQualityTests, AtmosphereMultipleScatteringTests and
    AtmosphereComputationTests selection passed 33/33 in both Debug and Release, with no
    failures or skips. Test-process wall times were 48.173s / 15.905s; separate builds took
    110.380s / 107.480s. Summed case durations were 76.940s / 17.555s; CPU test classes can
    overlap, so those sums are not elapsed selection time. Source hashes remained unchanged.
    These are single qualifying baseline runs per configuration, not an optimization comparison.
    Additional repeat receipts are excluded because their execution/source provenance was not
    reconciled. Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/
    Phase3-atmosphere/summary.json, configuration-specific TRX files and commands-and-conditions.txt.
    Traceability: the shared performance analyses above govern preserved observations,
    ownership and measurement. [PBR.Atmosphere.md](PBR.Atmosphere.md), Multiple scattering
    and Publication and rendering, supplies immutable-table, quality-budget and completed-work
    admission requirements. AtmosphereComputationTests.ControlledProducer demonstrates the
    existing completion seam; AtmosphereLookup and AtmosphereComputation remain unchanged.
    The user approved the following separation on 2026-11-03. Keep
    QualityOnlyEditRebuildsLighting as the real quality-0-to-1
    integration witness for table/snapshot replacement, dimensions and revision. Convert only
    AsyncCompletionAdmitsQualityOnlyChange to controlled completion, checking admitted request
    inputs/quality, publication identity/order, replacement admission and stable-input reuse.
    Share the three immutable default aerosol tables between SourceRemainsFiniteAndNightIsDark
    and DefaultQuadratureAgreesWithRefinement through a bounded class fixture keyed by every
    determining input. Retain all aerosol rows, refined builds, quality comparisons, independent
    references and tolerances. The implemented separation removes two full builds from the
    async policy test and reduces the two numerical methods' default-table builds from six to
    three; these are source-derived operation counts, not instrumented runtime counters.
    All three refined integrations remain. The async test now covers scheduling independently
    of numerical integration; the synchronous test retains the real quality-change witness.
    AtmosphereMultipleScatteringTableFixture owns at most three immutable managed tables for
    one test class, keys all eight determining numerical inputs and releases its references
    at class teardown. No production code or test parameter rows changed.
  Implementation verification and acceptance (2026-11-03)
    Corrected Debug and Release selections each passed 33/33, with no failures or skips and
    exactly the baseline case-name sets. Test-process wall times were 33.236s / 11.335s;
    summed case durations were 55.216s / 13.129s. Separate builds passed in 20.499s / 18.104s,
    with zero errors. Each numerical method also passed its three aerosol rows alone in a
    fresh Debug process (7.992s / 29.162s), confirming no prerequisite test order.
    The controlled test asserts frozen quality-0/1 requests, pending ownership, completion
    publication order/identity and no repeated stable-input admission. The real synchronous
    integration still checks replaced table/snapshot identity, width 64 and revision 2.
    Numerical equivalence follows unchanged Build inputs, immutable read-only sampling and
    the retained independent refined comparisons, sample matrices and tolerances. Test source
    hashes were stable throughout corrected verification; production source hashes match the
    baseline. No production cache, collection parallelism or shared mutable lookup was added.
    Baseline/candidate used the same three-class filter, UnitTests.runsettings, package cache
    and sequential fresh CPU test processes; existing build/filesystem caches were retained.
    Each configuration has one qualifying baseline and one corrected candidate. Both current
    candidate walls are lower, but these sequential single samples do not establish a stable
    percentage improvement or whole-suite savings. CPU synchronization and GPU execution were
    not separately measured; the selected work is CPU-only. Summed class times can overlap.
    An earlier Debug run passed 32/33 using a pre-correction assertion that incorrectly expected
    a previously consumed result again. Its failure receipt is retained separately and excluded
    from the final result; the corrected test expects no new publication on unchanged input.
    Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/Phase3-atmosphere-postchange-corrected/
    summary.json, Debug/Release and isolated-method TRX files, commands and source hash manifests.
    Second review and independent implementation/coverage audit are complete, with no required
    code corrections remaining. The user accepted these measured results and limitations on
    2026-11-03. No staging or commit was performed.
